*** guenomu *** Importance Sampling (IS) for two gene families assuming gene tree is known with certainty.

Example execution of the program (serial version):

$ guenomu run_importance_sampling.ctrl
$ guenomu analyze_output.ctrl

(parallel version):

$ mpirun -np 4 guenomu run_importance_sampling.ctrl
$ mpirun -np 4 guenomu analyze_output.ctrl

The output files of interest would be job0.params.txt (with sampled parameters), job0.species.tre (with rooted species
trees ordered by posterior frequency) and job0.unrooted.tre (species trees by posterior frequency, if we neglect the
root location and assume the trees are unrooted). Notice that the unrooted trees are still represented as rooted ones,
please neglect the apparent root location.
_________


This example data set contains two gene families (downloaded from http://www.treefam.org/ and edited), TF354246.tre and
TF354258.tre, both with only one gene tree per family. This is interpreted as gene family trees known without error --
the alternative would be several trees per family, with their frequencies representing their relative posterior
probabilities from sequence-based analysis.

The control file for running the IS analysis under "guenomu" is called  "run_importance_sampling.ctrl", which contains
the number of iterations for the main MCMC procedure, for the "mini-sampler" of auxiliary gene trees when updating the
species tree, etc. This control control file also tells that the list of species names is in file  "list_of_species.txt"
and that the list of gene family tree files is described in "list_of_trees.txt".

The output goes to a checkpoint file in binary format [1], that needs another call to guenomu with the control file
"analyze_output.ctrl". Notice that this file is exactly the same as "run_importance_sampling.ctrl" but with 
"param_execute_action = analyse" [2]. If you use the MPI version of the IS sampler, you must also use MPI with the same
number of processes for analyzing the output.


Notes: 
[1] The checkpoint file is called job0.checkpoint.bin for the serial version and job${i}.checkpoint.bin for the parallel
version (where $i is the job number). Therefore currently we must use the same parallel parameters when analysing a
parallel analysis (that is, same number of processes). An alternative (not implemented yet) is to give explicitely the
checkpoint file names, which can be used to study convergence... (the control file already supports this, but nowadays
is neglected by program). The MCMC analysis does not need to be completed for these checkpoint files to be read -- since
they are checkpoints, they are robust to a sudden halt of the analysis. 

[2] In the future I may remove the "execute_action" from the control file and leave it as a command line option so that
the exact same control file can be used for both. Some parameters do not matter (like number of iterations, prior
values, etc.) but the species names and list of gene tree must be the same -- unpredictable errors may arise.

